---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-sendlane';
const title = 'Drip vs Sendlane for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip SMS access and Sendlane continuity under Privy for Shopify campaigns, permission, inventory, purchase exits and contribution.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "An email-led team can evaluate coordinated Shopify journeys if its account qualifies for Drip SMS. Confirm staged US access, paid-plan and Support All People requirements and email-linked phone identity."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Sendlane fit?",
    "answer": "An existing Sendlane team may benefit from continuity of its established workflows and service relationship under Privy. Confirm its actual account, contract and migration path before changing senders."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the Privy acquisition guarantee parity?",
    "answer": "No. The acquisition announcement does not establish account-level feature, sender, pricing, reporting or migration parity. Request written scope and test exports and rebuilt flows."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a purchase authorize promotional SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Keep Shopify purchase history separate from channel and purpose permission. Preserve consent evidence and revocations, and test purchase exits before releasing queued promotions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should another email tool be added?",
    "answer": "Only for a defined gap with one owner per journey. An existing Sequenzy education sequence can remain during evaluation if entry rules, exclusions and reporting prevent duplicate sends."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use current written quotes for the same eligible audience and send mix. Compare incremental contribution per eligible customer against a comparable holdout, including operating and migration costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Drip vs Sendlane</nav>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Sendlane for Shopify campaigns</h1>
    <p class="mt-7">Start with the account you can operate today: Drip’s current SMS scope and Sendlane’s continuity under Privy determine which campaign plans are feasible.</p>
    <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
      { label: 'Email-led journeys', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Evaluate when SMS approval and email-linked identity fit the audience.' },
      { label: 'Existing operation', tool: 'Sendlane / Privy', reason: 'Evaluate continuity with written account scope and tested transition controls.' },
    ]} />
    <article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current scope before campaign design</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip’s SMS page</a> describes staged access for existing US customers on paid plans with Support All People enabled. Phones require an email; SMS-only profiles are unsupported. It describes toll-free, one-way sending rather than keyword joining, short codes or two-way conversations. Check account approval before planning a text-led drop. The <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">SMS overview</a> documents broadcasts and workflow messages; validate the message type and controls your account can actually use.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane in February 2026</a>, describing Sendlane as an email and SMS platform with a service-first approach. This supports evaluating continuity for an existing Sendlane operation. It does not prove that a new Privy account reproduces every Sendlane workflow. Confirm the current product brand, account access, support owner, sender continuity, export rights and migration obligations in writing.</p>
      <h2>Identity, permission and purchase exits</h2>
      <p>Map Shopify customer IDs, normalized email and phone, order IDs and event timestamps to destination profiles. Test guest checkout, changed email, shared phones and duplicate events without silently merging unrelated people. A synced phone or order is not promotional permission: keep channel, purpose, capture source, timestamp, disclosure version and revocations independently traceable.</p>
<p>Assign one team to consent and suppression reconciliation, one owner to each journey and one owner to incident recovery. Apply channel-specific opt-outs and purpose restrictions at entry and immediately before sending. A global do-not-contact request needs an explicit rule across tools; an SMS opt-out must not be treated as permission to message elsewhere. Validate local scheduling and quiet-hour behavior for supported destinations with qualified advice rather than relying on a universal time window.</p>
<p>Propose tests for purchase before a queued reminder, cancellation, refund, delayed order arrival and repeated webhook delivery. Record expected exits for each event type. Deduplicate on stable event IDs, handle out-of-order timestamps and make replay idempotent. Reconcile Shopify orders and consent records against destination entries, suppressions and sends before resuming a failed job; quarantine unresolved identities rather than replaying every promotion.</p>
      <h2>Proposed merchant campaign tests</h2>
      <p>The named scenarios below are planning examples retained from this comparison, not documented customers, executed tests or measured results. Each needs eligible recipients, reliable inventory and purchase exits before sending.</p>
<ul>
<li>Bloom &amp; Wick: prepare holiday teasers and shipping-deadline tiers ahead of the season. Separate gift-bundle messages from self-purchase scent education; an existing Sequenzy email sequence needs a named owner and shared exclusions.</li>
<li>Ridge Run Coffee: exclude active subscription customers from flash offers; test late subscription updates before admitting engaged non-subscribers.</li>
<li>Cipher Audio: compare a teaser email followed by permissioned browse-interest launch texts with a restrained alternative. Keep full-price messaging as a hypothesis; measure contribution rather than claiming a discount-free margin gain.</li>
<li>Patchwork Denim: route one-of-one listing drops by explicit style preferences and stop sends when the specific piece sells.</li>
<li>Arena Energy: coordinate a flavor and athlete collaboration for the relevant audience; reserve a proposed shaker incentive for email while text communicates timing, with collision checks.</li>
<li>Loom &amp; Frame: disclose edition size and test collector notifications at a verified sell-through threshold. Define collector eligibility from order history and validate stock immediately before release.</li>
<li>Driftwood Surf Co: coordinate seasonal drops with swell-forecast education and compare restock waitlist targeting with a broader eligible audience.</li>
<li>Noir Collective: schedule a recurring drop with a defined VIP lead window; test lower broadcast frequency without assuming higher revenue per send.</li>
<li>SoleDrop ATX: preserve size-specific waitlist priority, issue controlled early links and release general-list email only when remaining inventory supports it.</li>
<li>Festival Threads Co: align city-specific tour messages with venue timezones and impose a documented per-city campaign cap.</li>
<li>Vinyl Vault Records: disclose pressing quantity and distinguish waitlist members, repeat collectors and value segments without letting past spend override permission.</li>
<li>Peak Trail Gear: isolate bargain-sensitive clearance recipients from full-price loyalists. Use a comparable holdout to assess whether incentives add contribution.</li>
<li>Sauce Lab: use verified batch inventory to trigger a scarcity message with a per-drop cap, cancelling queued alerts after purchase or sellout.</li>
<li>Ember Ceramics: preserve kiln-release waitlist and fulfillment order; pair maker-story email with a restrained VIP access text only where each purpose is authorized.</li>
</ul>
      <h2>Contribution, quotes and a release decision</h2>
      <p>Request comparable peak-season quotes covering email audience, SMS subscribers, message segments, destinations, number provisioning, support, overages and migration work. Drip’s <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48926725054349-How-We-Calculate-SMS-Billing">billing documentation</a> describes subscriber and segment-based usage tiers with carrier fees included; do not add an assumed carrier surcharge again. Ask the Sendlane/Privy account owner which billing terms apply to the actual account.</p>
<p>For a comparable eligible-customer cohort, calculate contribution as net sales after discounts and returns minus product cost, variable fulfillment, messaging, incremental platform costs and support. Deduct discounts and returns once. Compare contribution per eligible customer with a holdout over an observation window that fits the purchase and return cycle. Separate one-time migration work from recurring costs and report uncertainty; attributed revenue alone is not incremental lift.</p>
<p>Drip is a conditional fit for email-led coordination where its SMS access and profile model match the store. Sendlane continuity is a conditional fit where the existing account, service relationship and documented Privy transition support the required workflows. Choose after identity, permission, suppression, purchase-exit and reconciliation tests pass, with an accountable operator and rollback plan. No blanket winner or fixed pilot duration follows from these examples.</p>
      <p>Related operating references: <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent evidence</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>. Sources checked November 25, 2026; confirm current account terms before release.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Sendlane FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
